{
  "concept": "A polar rectangle R is the region swept between two constant-radius arcs and two constant-angle rays, and it is the natural building block for double integration in polar coordinates.",
  "figureType": "diagram",
  "elements": [
    "Horizontal black baseline representing the polar axis (positive x-axis), with the pole/origin at its left end",
    "Dashed red ray from the origin at angle theta = pi/6 (lower bounding ray), extending past the outer arc",
    "Dashed red ray from the origin at angle theta = pi/4 (upper bounding ray), extending past the outer arc",
    "Solid blue circular arc of radius r = 1.5 (inner arc), drawn over an angular span wider than the region so its ends stick out past both rays",
    "Solid blue circular arc of radius r = 2 (outer arc), drawn similarly over-extended",
    "Light-blue shaded curved quadrilateral R bounded by the two arcs and two rays",
    "Label 'r = 1.5' with leader pointing to the inner arc",
    "Label 'r = 2' with leader pointing to the outer arc",
    "Label 'theta = pi/6' placed below/right along the lower dashed ray",
    "Label 'theta = pi/4' placed left along the upper dashed ray",
    "Italic label 'R' centered inside the shaded region",
    "Optional overlay: dashed grey straight-sided rectangle of dimensions delta-r by r*delta-theta anchored at the inner-lower corner of R",
    "Optional overlay annotations: delta-r radial thickness bracket, r*delta-theta arc-length bracket, and area expression",
    "Faint polar grid of concentric circles and radial spokes for context"
  ],
  "interactions": [
    {
      "id": "innerRadius",
      "type": "slider",
      "teaches": "The inner boundary of a polar rectangle is a curve of constant r, so moving it slides a circular arc rather than a straight line.",
      "label": "Inner radius r₁",
      "range": [0.2, 2.8, 0.05],
      "default": 1.5
    },
    {
      "id": "outerRadius",
      "type": "slider",
      "teaches": "Δr = r₂ − r₁ controls the radial thickness of R; as r₂ → r₁ the region collapses to an arc.",
      "label": "Outer radius r₂",
      "range": [0.3, 3.0, 0.05],
      "default": 2.0
    },
    {
      "id": "startAngle",
      "type": "slider",
      "teaches": "Holding r fixed and rotating the lower ray sweeps R around the pole without changing its radial extent.",
      "label": "Start angle θ₁ (× π)",
      "range": [0, 0.45, 0.0104166],
      "default": 0.1666667
    },
    {
      "id": "endAngle",
      "type": "slider",
      "teaches": "Δθ = θ₂ − θ₁ controls the angular width; the outer edge grows faster than the inner edge because arc length is r·Δθ.",
      "label": "End angle θ₂ (× π)",
      "range": [0.02, 0.5, 0.0104166],
      "default": 0.25
    },
    {
      "id": "cartesianOverlay",
      "type": "toggle",
      "teaches": "R is only an analogue of a rectangle: a true Δr × rΔθ rectangle matches it at a corner but its straight sides peel away from the circular arcs, and the mismatch shrinks as R shrinks.",
      "label": "Overlay straight Δr × rΔθ rectangle",
      "options": ["off", "on"],
      "default": "off"
    }
  ],
  "demo_steps": [
    {
      "title": "The region as drawn",
      "narration": "The shaded region R is trapped between the circular arcs r = 1.5 and r = 2 and the rays θ = π/6 and θ = π/4. Its four sides come from just two equations: r = constant and θ = constant.",
      "state": { "innerRadius": 1.5, "outerRadius": 2.0, "startAngle": 0.1666667, "endAngle": 0.25, "cartesianOverlay": "off" }
    },
    {
      "title": "Change the radial bounds",
      "narration": "Push the arcs apart while keeping the rays fixed. R grows only in the radial direction — the curved sides slide outward but the straight sides stay on the same two rays.",
      "state": { "innerRadius": 1.0, "outerRadius": 2.8, "startAngle": 0.1666667, "endAngle": 0.25, "cartesianOverlay": "off" }
    },
    {
      "title": "Change the angular bounds",
      "narration": "Now fix the radii and open the rays. R sweeps through a wider wedge, and notice the outer edge lengthens more than the inner edge — arc length is r·Δθ, so it depends on how far out you are.",
      "state": { "innerRadius": 1.5, "outerRadius": 2.0, "startAngle": 0.0833333, "endAngle": 0.4166667, "cartesianOverlay": "off" }
    },
    {
      "title": "Compare with a true rectangle",
      "narration": "Overlay a straight-sided rectangle of the same Δr by rΔθ dimensions at the inner corner. Its flat sides visibly miss the arcs — R is curved, not a real rectangle.",
      "state": { "innerRadius": 1.5, "outerRadius": 2.0, "startAngle": 0.1666667, "endAngle": 0.4166667, "cartesianOverlay": "on" }
    },
    {
      "title": "Shrink it toward a differential element",
      "narration": "Make Δr and Δθ small. The straight rectangle and the curved region become indistinguishable, which is exactly why dA = r dr dθ works inside a double integral.",
      "state": { "innerRadius": 1.7, "outerRadius": 1.85, "startAngle": 0.1666667, "endAngle": 0.2083333, "cartesianOverlay": "on" }
    }
  ],
  "keyInsight": "A polar rectangle isn't a rectangle — two of its sides are arcs of length r·Δθ — but as Δr and Δθ shrink it behaves like a Δr by rΔθ rectangle, which is where the factor r in dA = r dr dθ comes from.",
  "reconstructionNotes": "Pole at the lower-left, horizontal black polar axis running right. Two thin dashed red rays leave the pole at the two angles and extend well past the outer arc. Two thick solid blue circular arcs centered at the pole, drawn a bit wider than the angular span so their ends visibly overrun the rays (as in the original). The enclosed curved quadrilateral is filled pale blue with a bold italic serif 'R' at its centroid. Labels in serif math style: 'r = 1.5' and 'r = 2' to the upper-left of each arc with short leader lines, 'θ = π/6' below the lower ray and 'θ = π/4' to the left of the upper ray, both with stacked fractions. Labels should update live to the slider values (show θ as a multiple of π). When the overlay toggle is on, draw a grey dashed straight-sided rectangle anchored at the inner-lower corner of R, width Δr along the θ₁ direction and height r₁·Δθ perpendicular to it, with small bracket annotations 'Δr' and 'r Δθ' and a caption 'area ≈ r Δr Δθ'. Keep a very faint polar grid (concentric circles at integer radii, spokes every π/12) behind everything for orientation."
}

